#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064020 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064376 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0cf1bf7-69a3-50b2-adaa-84982ffac95e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Side Given the Rate of Change of the Volume"}] :question/id #uuid "1c2e1a0e-4e7b-5029-94c3-3eb14d8869bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-222" :question/problem "A cube is shrinking uniformly at a rate of $3\\,\\text{mm}^3/\\text{s}.$ How fast is each side of the cube shrinking when the volume of the cube is $27\\,\\text{mm}^3?$\n\n![](images/0c/0ca7021995b1a501f6cb862b33649bdb1927f7ce4a8630a9e261382a193f40a4.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side of the cube and $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}$ when $V=27\\,\\text{mm}^3.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV=x^3\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V = 27\\,\\text{mm}^3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 27 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=3$ and $\\dfrac{\\textrm d V}{\\textrm dt}=-3$ into the expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -3 & =3(3)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sides of the cube are shrinking at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{9}\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=3\\,\\textrm{mm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064021 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c29b3f9-5eac-5272-9715-f300211f91ad" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Surface Area Given the Rate of Change of a Side (and Vice Versa)"}] :question/id #uuid "74034d81-c90c-5ab4-b27c-aa1d7aca70cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-223" :question/problem "All of the edges of a cube are increasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}.$ How fast is the surface area of the cube changing when the volume of the cube is $8\\,\\text{cm}^3?$\n\n![](images/a6/a6ef2af1ed8e44f82469625e232ed67c0b809a3c8199a6ce52fd2bf2e3fcffa7.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d S}{\\textrm dt}$ when $V= 8\\,\\text{cm}^3.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS= 6x^2\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}S & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V= 8\\,\\text{cm}^3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 8 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=2\\,\\text{cm}=20\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nWe can now plug the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12(20)(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =480.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cube's surface area is changing at a rate of  $480 \\,\\text{mm}^2/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064022 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064378 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814ed4fa-33c3-57eb-b843-6268c818fe36" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Volume Given the Rate of Change of a Side"}] :question/id #uuid "6694f63e-e83a-5597-81cb-80af3c132524" :question/math-academy-id "e-224" :question/problem "Water is being poured into a rectangular prism-shaped tank. The length and width of this tank are $20 \\textrm{ ft}$ and $15 \\textrm{ ft},$ respectively. If the height of the water increases at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{6}\\,\\textrm{ft/min}$, determine the rate at which the volume of stored water increases.\n\n![](images/ea/ea7c6749ec0585ab714640fa9155eba212e2a9c628b50cc3c3de07492b8b3aec.png)" :question/worked-solution "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the tank, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the water in the tank, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=20\\,\\text{ft}, \\qquad  W=15\\,\\text{ft}, \\qquad  \\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt} = \\dfrac{1}{6}\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dV}{\\textrm dt}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of the water in the tank:\n\n$$\nV = LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(20)(15)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =300\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is rising at a rate of $50\\,\\text{ft}^3\\textrm{/min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064376 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0cf1bf7-69a3-50b2-adaa-84982ffac95e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Side Given the Rate of Change of the Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027d65cd-408d-54f0-8851-920431c6df14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500475} {:answer/id #uuid "3f077957-2111-50ad-b8a3-980fad8a395e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{17}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500590} {:answer/id #uuid "57e11df3-0c32-5640-afed-b16fcbb6e9cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{3}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500646} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f1f4a6-e404-5f43-830e-76e2bf630422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500893} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e27c78-c668-58d3-9f71-075b75e4b8fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8f1f4a6-e404-5f43-830e-76e2bf630422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500893} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8097c0f-208a-5485-aa29-effca04536fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b7d80eb-f47e-553b-8d7c-69b51e01b399" :question/math-academy-id "q-116080" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e11ec3379c969bbc8499f36978f3a39efe1342ee6563569949dbcb61611ab5f4.png)\n\nA cube of ice is melting uniformly at a rate of $204\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min},$ where $x$ is the length of its edge. How fast is each edge of the ice block decreasing when the volume of the cube is $216\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $V$ be the volume of the ice cube. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-204\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $V=216\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\n  We start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n  \n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBefore we plug in the known values, we need to find the value of $x$ when $V=216\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{3} \\\\ 216 & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=6$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-204$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -204 & =3(6)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{17}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sides of the cube are decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{17}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=6\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c29b3f9-5eac-5272-9715-f300211f91ad" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Surface Area Given the Rate of Change of a Side (and Vice Versa)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "123f9b2b-0563-52eb-a8a6-c11e35140af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500507} {:answer/id #uuid "31c4c2e2-f9c7-55fa-9b0b-965e7a60983f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500570} {:answer/id #uuid "6a44857f-c227-5ee8-907e-759c13f62a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500686} {:answer/id #uuid "d4901436-e9f5-5dcb-ad95-a9bd329e5817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500920} {:answer/id #uuid "ee63a80b-6b4b-5e42-89f8-7c9624056a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a44857f-c227-5ee8-907e-759c13f62a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500686} :answer-field/id #uuid "360e3f58-2f52-52b9-85ca-0ee548100f6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6798c7d5-6dc4-50ce-934a-27d8986f8abb" :question/math-academy-id "q-116498" :question/problem "![](images/32/324e3b2752fd398e47ce066dfc44f8a4d96cc3d7916f917fa142485ae86bf48f.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ the width is increasing at a rate of $11\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ and the height is decreasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.$ How fast is the surface area increasing when the dimensions of the rectangular prism are $2\\,\\text{cm}$ in length, $4\\,\\text{cm}$ in width and $7\\,\\text{cm}$ in height?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $S$ be its surface area. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =4\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =7\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =11\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x,$ $y$ and $z.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2xz+2xy+2yz) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2x+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+(2x+2z)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(2z+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 4)(-5)+(2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 7)(11)+(2\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 4)(-5) \\\\  & =-60+198-110 \\\\  & =28\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the solid is increasing at a rate of $28\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c29b3f9-5eac-5272-9715-f300211f91ad" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Surface Area Given the Rate of Change of a Side (and Vice Versa)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d9da049-a5de-5ea0-9fe8-5b987a55d80e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500563} {:answer/id #uuid "3538c791-2780-512a-91df-ad5c36e93cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500578} {:answer/id #uuid "46d896d9-f073-535d-b7d9-9558f591b993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500607} {:answer/id #uuid "5013e2ef-3020-5d40-a3be-1725d68decf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500631} {:answer/id #uuid "757d12dc-4e03-5cbe-aa1a-74dced4f3122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "757d12dc-4e03-5cbe-aa1a-74dced4f3122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500711} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2eb92cf-2f1b-5649-bd7d-f3661cb6b3bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38e9b5c8-f7c2-5d27-9b07-5dff34e427b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-116519" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7aad5aea708422b331b19ab2e9c7586b5b037054347841530db829e2fc3c9075.png)\n\nIf the surface area of a cube is increasing at a rate of $144{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ at what rate are the edges changing when each edge is $4\\text{ cm}?$ Assume that each edge is increasing at the same rate." :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=144\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=4\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $x$ knowing the rate of change of $S.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(S) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 144 & =12(4)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the edges are changing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c29b3f9-5eac-5272-9715-f300211f91ad" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Surface Area Given the Rate of Change of a Side (and Vice Versa)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77e02e51-9520-5960-af4b-5e7149ff91fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500715} {:answer/id #uuid "da1699ce-9cb8-5975-b914-ea145e4e7b1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500931} {:answer/id #uuid "e96e67b0-5d53-5ccd-92b7-40b07cd5c7a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500967} {:answer/id #uuid "f20456ef-886c-5102-993e-3508d86e67a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500980} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b001ca-ac85-5dbf-8676-ca2181df987d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f20456ef-886c-5102-993e-3508d86e67a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500980} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d9ddee8-9080-52f3-b380-7069a58028d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e4521f6-9e6d-5c0e-807a-ed8494a208e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-2775" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7aad5aea708422b331b19ab2e9c7586b5b037054347841530db829e2fc3c9075.png)\n\nIf the surface area of a cube is increasing at a rate of $48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ at what rate are the edges changing when each edge is $2\\text{ cm}?$ Assume that each edge is increasing at the same rate." :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=48\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=2\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $x$ knowing the rate of change of $S.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(S) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 48 & =12(2)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the edges are changing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064376 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0cf1bf7-69a3-50b2-adaa-84982ffac95e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Side Given the Rate of Change of the Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f044fe5-a78a-503c-8136-573bff49033a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500531} {:answer/id #uuid "2903205c-6d62-59f7-9321-c5aced82f76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500545} {:answer/id #uuid "31598524-9782-5a60-9550-6527d0e8e332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500569} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd5b608-b1ff-5a9d-9abd-12c753eba961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500731} {:answer/id #uuid "d37f3eb9-f956-5d80-bd59-824978a5c588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31598524-9782-5a60-9550-6527d0e8e332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500569} :answer-field/id #uuid "f60c8b13-b572-54f8-b997-4b367f683cdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a02e865a-6a6d-5b10-8171-4b544dbd46b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-2795" :question/problem "![](images/52/52448daf1f9aea06b4ab4713756080ddb3ab465b4fae8f92b65a54c251a81d5e.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing by $8\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}$ and its height increasing at a rate of $1\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ If the volume of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $50\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min},$  how fast is its width changing when its dimensions are $10\\,\\text{cm}$ in length, $15\\,\\text{cm}$ in width and $5\\,\\text{cm}$ in height?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =10\\text{ cm}, \\\\ y & =5\\text{ cm}, \\\\ z & =15\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-50{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=10\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=5\\,\\text{cm},$ and $z=15\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\cdot y\\cdot z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =xy(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+xz(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+yz(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-50 & =(10)(5)(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+(10)(15)(1)+(15)(5)(-8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =8\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is increasing at a rate of $8\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=10\\,\\text{cm},$ $y=5\\,\\text{cm},$ and $z=15\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064724 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064378 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814ed4fa-33c3-57eb-b843-6268c818fe36" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Volume Given the Rate of Change of a Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275c62d3-3e00-52d0-9d98-f55885e3b40d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "440\\,{\\text{feet}}^{3}\\text{/hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500544} {:answer/id #uuid "4f841d86-51bb-5ddc-8524-7fe2a6b1ca71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "240\\,{\\text{feet}}^{3}\\text{/hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500629} {:answer/id #uuid "5c73fdcc-2e08-5492-82ae-b65c09e57968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "400\\,{\\text{feet}}^{3}\\text{/hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500657} {:answer/id #uuid "5c8adc1b-e870-5266-9de9-84b7d51f9fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300\\,{\\text{feet}}^{3}\\text{/hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500658} {:answer/id #uuid "d7cfb825-dab5-5d3d-a6ab-f334d5939ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "360\\,{\\text{feet}}^{3}\\text{/hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c8adc1b-e870-5266-9de9-84b7d51f9fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300\\,{\\text{feet}}^{3}\\text{/hour}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500658} :answer-field/id #uuid "f954cd2b-8873-5841-b890-5fd3add8628e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51e84601-668c-5024-8729-dea441ebbfac" :question/math-academy-id "q-30022" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f504ea91bae5d27b3e7b31d697ed06c9e34d838724e8dafac46e69d84de9c3cf.png)\n\nA gas station has a rectangular gasoline storage tank stored underground. The tank is $15\\text{ feet}$ long and $10\\text{ feet}$ wide. A worker lowers a dip stick into the tank and notices that the depth of the gasoline is decreasing by $2\\text{ feet}$ every hour. How fast is the volume of gasoline decreasing?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the tank, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the gasoline in the tank, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=15\\,\\text{ft},\\,W=10\\,\\text{ft},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{hour}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of the gasoline in the tank:\n\n$$\nV=LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWh$ with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(15)(10)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =150\\cdot (-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-300.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gasoline level is decreasing at a rate of $300\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/hour}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064378 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814ed4fa-33c3-57eb-b843-6268c818fe36" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Volume Given the Rate of Change of a Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1b4802-4fbf-597a-8717-edd9da5ff601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500498} {:answer/id #uuid "1d66b81f-aab7-5dee-bc76-93149d565137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500528} {:answer/id #uuid "651e5094-ed0f-5287-afa0-88760a288cd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44\\,100{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500678} {:answer/id #uuid "70ef183c-3e32-589d-bdef-3a17eb486140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,150{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500700} {:answer/id #uuid "c387d300-dcb3-5156-be2d-bccac09b58bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d66b81f-aab7-5dee-bc76-93149d565137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500528} :answer-field/id #uuid "7049e9d7-8f84-594a-912b-f5fcab69cbfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d8efcf9-a827-5e33-8982-8c950b1390fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-30081" :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea7c818a6694ba959c5230dcde8611a7b1dc44bcad383f54c93d2934336c35b6.png)\n\nThe edges of a cube decrease at a rate of $9\\text{ cm/s}.$ At what rate is the volume decreasing when the length of each edge is $40\\text{ cm}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side length of the cube and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-9\\text{ cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=40\\text{ cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the cube:\n\n$$\nV={x}^{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug $x=40$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-9\\text{ cm/s}$ into the expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3{x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3(40)^{2}\\cdot (-9)\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-43\\,200.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the cube is decreasing at a rate of $43\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}$ at the moment when $x=40\\,\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064376 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0cf1bf7-69a3-50b2-adaa-84982ffac95e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Side Given the Rate of Change of the Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb50cfa7-d5fd-5e1c-bd95-f2d2d5a2cd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb50cfa7-d5fd-5e1c-bd95-f2d2d5a2cd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500970} :answer-field/id #uuid "4367237c-774c-57e1-b55c-8026f7a5b4ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b06ab3d9-3b8c-5c9f-a694-75f899defb02" :question/math-academy-id "q-314513" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7b45121fc7e3b6de3e37053b944bed610e55d337b7c73d89b258a89f903ad0c2.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing by $1.5\\text{ feet}\\text{/min}$ and its height is increasing at a rate of $1\\text{ feet/min}.$ If the volume of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $9{\\text{ feet}}^{3}\\text{/min},$ how fast is its width decreasing when its dimensions are $5\\,\\text{feet}$ in length, $3\\,\\text{feet}$ in width, and $2\\,\\text{feet}$ in height? Assume that the rate of decrease of width is positive.\n\nThe width is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{feet}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\,\\text{feet}, \\\\ y & =3\\,\\text{feet}, \\\\ z & =2\\,\\text{feet}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5\\,\\text{feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\,\\text{feet}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-9\\,{\\text{feet}}^{3}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=5\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=3\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=2\\,\\text{feet}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x\\cdot y\\cdot z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =xy(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+xz(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+yz(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9 & =(5)(3)(1)+(5)(2)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(2)(3)(-1.5) \\\\ -9 & =15+10\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})-9 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the solid is decreasing at a rate of $1.5\\,\\text{feet}/\\text{min}$ at the moment when $x=5\\,\\text{feet},$ $y=2\\,\\text{feet},$ and $z=3\\,\\text{feet}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064378 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814ed4fa-33c3-57eb-b843-6268c818fe36" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Volume Given the Rate of Change of a Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfb0f980-6010-5ae0-ac8a-7691fa64a449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "153.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfb0f980-6010-5ae0-ac8a-7691fa64a449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "153.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500914} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb3869d-baa4-5c43-ac49-35af5641a53b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94a70c07-52ed-54e0-b434-f6f0663ba853" :question/math-academy-id "q-314534" :question/problem "![](images/81/81175de5197dac2f2f037bde6985be619c68de75a574853c61de75316ae63971.png)\n\nA rectangular swimming pool is being drained. If its length and width are $32\\text{ m}$ and $12\\text{ m}$, respectively, and the water level is falling at a rate of $0.4\\,\\text{m/min},$ how fast is the water draining out of the pool? Assume that the drain rate is positive and do not round the answer.\n\nThe water is draining out at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$" :question/worked-solution "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the swimming pool, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the water, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=32\\,\\text{m},\\,W=12\\,\\text{m},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-0.4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of water:\n\n$$\nV=LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWh$ with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(32)(12)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =384\\cdot (-0.4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-153.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water is draining out at a rate of $153.6\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064376 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0cf1bf7-69a3-50b2-adaa-84982ffac95e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Side Given the Rate of Change of the Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "442e3b6f-0334-59dc-bbe2-9c521ab09118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "442e3b6f-0334-59dc-bbe2-9c521ab09118" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500602} :answer-field/id #uuid "41fb01ad-fc9d-5c1d-96c5-0e78cb67f17e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3d27d1e-a375-5722-8f78-27b79ebf66c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-343180" :question/problem "![](images/49/4946691a4258fb310ec7052f1edd5a1b9117ca7daeea152e4a1b01b11a624e2e.png)\n\nJennie fills an aquarium, in the shape of a rectangular prism that is $50\\,\\text{cm}$ long and $25\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, with water from a tap. What is the rate of change of the water level if the volume is increasing at a rate of $250\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{h}?$\n\nThe water level is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{h}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the rectangular prism, let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =50\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =25\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=250\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=50\\,\\text{cm}$ and $y=25\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\nV=(50)(25)z=1250z.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1250z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =1250(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known value into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}250 & =1250(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =0.2\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is increasing at a rate of $0.2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064376 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0cf1bf7-69a3-50b2-adaa-84982ffac95e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Side Given the Rate of Change of the Volume"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9e53027-3a83-5961-948b-e0b5df7093f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9e53027-3a83-5961-948b-e0b5df7093f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501000} :answer-field/id #uuid "b852a09c-ea6b-5010-b9b3-a3b637cbd328" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4e15d3e-925b-56d4-bf57-253dd32acaf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-343194" :question/problem "![](images/99/996768e4b1ce8ae57bcf3cce61059f8349c307ea9f05c9c1f51831ff6fd8ae68.png)\n\nA rectangular loaf of rising bread dough is squeezed between two rigid side panels that keep its width fixed. The length of the loaf is decreasing by $20\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s},$ while the $60\\,\\text{mm}$ width does not change. If the volume of the loaf is constant, how fast is its height changing when its dimensions are $100\\,\\text{mm}$ in length and $30\\,\\text{mm}$ in height?\n\nThe height is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{mm}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =100\\,\\text{mm}, \\\\ y & =60\\,\\text{mm}, \\\\ z & =30\\,\\text{mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-20\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =0\\,{\\text{mm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=100\\,\\text{mm},$ $y=60\\,\\text{mm}$ and $z=30\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nWe start with the formula for the volume of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nSince $y$ is not changing and $y=60,$ we get\n\n$$\nV=60xz.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(60xz) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =60x(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+60z(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =60(100)(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})+60(30)(-20) \\\\ 0 & =6000(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})-36\\,000 \\\\ 36\\,000 & =6000(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =6\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the solid is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$ at the moment when $x=100\\,\\text{mm},$ $y=60\\,\\text{mm}$ and $z=30\\,\\text{mm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c29b3f9-5eac-5272-9715-f300211f91ad" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Surface Area Given the Rate of Change of a Side (and Vice Versa)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e1973b6-3fd1-50a3-a8c8-08ed11168d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e1973b6-3fd1-50a3-a8c8-08ed11168d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500727} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6d9c5f0-1b59-5800-900a-7031812188d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c009d1d-9f23-53f4-ac53-e3aab6d30ee8" :question/math-academy-id "q-343214" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c11298d151c2f93fbe7d00352386ff859f788ced2641ebe724fd7ddd9f7d205.png)\n\nA cube-shaped inflatable toy is being filled so that its surface area is growing at a rate of $150\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ How fast is its edge increasing when each edge is $5\\text{ in}?$\n\nThe cube's edge is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ be the side of the cube, let $S$ be its surface area, and let $V$ be its volume. All of the variables change with time. In particular, we are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=150\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$ when $x=5\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $x$ knowing the rate of change of $S.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the cube:\n\n$$\nS=6{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(S) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =12x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 150 & =12(5)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the edges are increasing at a rate of $2.5\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064378 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814ed4fa-33c3-57eb-b843-6268c818fe36" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Volume Given the Rate of Change of a Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5e820f6-11e5-54c5-8711-692747bbbe70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5e820f6-11e5-54c5-8711-692747bbbe70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500924} :answer-field/id #uuid "abea57b7-3c7f-5d2e-8706-848858e9de5a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68f6bf29-8273-5b7e-a834-752eee067f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-343321" :question/problem "![](images/ba/baa2d9cf6ae9d34de744374dbfe67e44589aa9331c1391e5fc168cd6a39bca4d.png)\n\nJennie fills an aquarium, in the shape of a rectangular prism that is $50\\,\\text{cm}$ long and $25\\,\\text{cm}$ wide, with water from a tap. How fast is the volume of water in the aquarium changing if the water level is rising at a rate of $0.2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{h}?$\n\nThe volume of water in the aquarium is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$" :question/worked-solution "We define the following variables:\n\n- let $L$ and $W$ be the length and width of the aquarium, respectively, and\n\n- let $h$ and $V$ be the height and volume of the water, respectively.\n\nNote that $h$ and $V$ change with time, while $L$ and $W$ are constants. \n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nL=50\\,\\text{m},\\,W=25\\,\\text{m},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=0.2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the two time-dependent variables, $V$ and $h,$ is given by the formula for the volume of water:\n\n$$\nV=LWh\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWh$ with respect to $t,$ treating both $L$ and $W$ as constants, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWh) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =LW\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(50)(25)\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =1250\\cdot 0.2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =250.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of water in the aquarium is increasing at a rate of $250\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064378 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "814ed4fa-33c3-57eb-b843-6268c818fe36" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Volume Given the Rate of Change of a Side"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d6b89b-9c2e-5338-b504-d4c40283ef6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "820{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500518} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2ce6b1-c4fc-5ed8-8a4c-3a4c5f749dd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "525{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500526} {:answer/id #uuid "864515c1-3b8e-59c9-b866-916b8262d2d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500747} {:answer/id #uuid "88a9531f-3fb7-5eed-9a5e-1179bfcc6d8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,750{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500755} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a2b408-e5cd-52e5-aaa7-f0316c914927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "670{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "864515c1-3b8e-59c9-b866-916b8262d2d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500747} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d21bae5-4b6c-54e9-aab3-a599790bd158" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd9599f1-ed67-5948-967c-4c445ad154d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-49012" :question/problem "![](images/06/06eb8269e36ecb0185c0bfa0069c36a705e36711ca0a705fba3e6eae7b6c946d.png)\n\nJohn is a carpenter who wants to modify a wooden box. The length, width and height of the box are decreased at a rate of $1\\text{ cm/s},$ $2\\text{ cm/s}$ and $4\\text{ cm/s},$ respectively. Determine the rate at which the volume of the box decreases, if its dimensions are $15\\text{ cm}$ long, $10\\text{ cm}$ wide and $15\\text{ cm}$ high." :question/worked-solution "Let $L,$ $W,$ $H$ and $V$ be the length, the width, the height, and the volume of the box, respectively. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L=15\\text{ cm},\\, & W=10\\text{ cm}, \\\\ H=15\\text{ cm},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t}=-1\\text{ cm/s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\text{ cm/s},\\, & \\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\text{ cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe relation between the four variables is given by the formula for the volume of a rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nV=LWH\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of $V=LWH$ with respect to $t,$ using the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}V & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(LWH) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =W\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot H(\\frac{\\text{d}W}{\\text{d}t})+L\\cdot W(\\frac{\\text{d}H}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plug in the known values and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =(10)(15)(-1)+(15)(15)(-2)+(15)(10)(-4) \\\\  & =-1\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the box is decreasing at a rate of $1\\,200{\\text{ cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0c29b3f9-5eac-5272-9715-f300211f91ad" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of the Surface Area Given the Rate of Change of a Side (and Vice Versa)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "463b2dfe-12dc-5d8b-b485-bbdc7bf1bfc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375546} {:answer/id #uuid "a530aa11-ef3e-5bcd-8caf-52f9a0d11205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375547} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb143ef-2ae2-5c0f-ad9f-f168411366e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375548} {:answer/id #uuid "df3affe2-648f-55b4-a71c-2fce585166f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375549} {:answer/id #uuid "5406e375-01d7-556f-a3db-50d6681e14cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdb143ef-2ae2-5c0f-ad9f-f168411366e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375548} :answer-field/id #uuid "05433573-3744-5b22-9ee4-30694c12c8a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c144d86d-616b-51ad-b91f-c522e265b17b" :question/math-academy-id "q-86066" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b2158da0465250d14ce47909e7f1ef918c06c9721547a3533e1c65797cc9fab8.png)\n\nThe length of a rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $3\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ the width is increasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min},$ and the $5\\,\\text{cm}$ height does not change. How fast is the surface area of the rectangular prism decreasing when its dimensions are $3\\,\\text{cm}$ in length and $10\\,\\text{cm}$ in width?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y,z$ be the dimensions of the solid, and let $V$ be its volume. We are given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ y & =10\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{cm}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-3\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =5\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe would like to find $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of $S$ knowing the rate of change of $x$ and $y.$ So, we start with the formula for the surface area of the rectangular prism:\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nSince $z=5\\,\\text{cm}$ is not changing, we get\n\n$$\nS=10x+2xy+10y.\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10x+2xy+10y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2x+10)(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})+(10+2y)(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plug in the known values into the above expression. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =(2\\cdot 3+10)(5)+(10+2\\cdot 10)(-3) \\\\  & =80-90 \\\\  & =-10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of the rectangular prism is decreasing at a rate of $10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$"}]}
